## Current file format

- 4 byte header: `LZW1`
- Each LZW code stored as fixed 16-bit / 2 byte value

Rough format:

```text
LZW1 | code | code | code | ...
       2B     2B     2B
```

## Initial compression test

Sample file:

```text
Original:   551 bytes
Compressed: 592 bytes
```

Difference:

```text
+41 bytes
```

Size reduction:

```text
-7.44%
```

So first build is actually larger than original.

## Why

Main issue:

- every LZW code currently uses 16 bits
- early dictionary codes only need around 9 bits
- small file also has header overhead

Example:

```text
294 codes × 16 bits
= 4704 bits
= 588 bytes

+ 4 byte header
= 592 bytes
```

So LZW algorithm works, but output storage is inefficient.

## Next improvement

Variable-width codes:

```text
0–511      -> 9 bits
512–1023   -> 10 bits
1024–2047  -> 11 bits
...
```

Need:

- bit writer
- bit reader
- new file format version, probably `LZW2`
- test width transitions
- compare compression stats before/ater 

  ## Need

- Bit writer
  - Packs 9-bit, 10-bit, 11-bit etc. LZW codes into normal 8-bit file bytes
  - Needed because variable-width codes do not line up neatly with bytes

- Bit reader
  - Reads the packed bytes back and reconstructs the original LZW codes
  - Must stay in sync with the writer's current code width

- Width tracking
  - Start at 9 bits
  - Move to 10 bits when dictionary reaches 512 entries
  - Then 11 bits at 1024, etc.

- New file format version
  - Change header from `LZW1` to `LZW2`
  - `LZW1` = fixed 16-bit codes
  - `LZW2` = variable-width packed codes

- More tests
  - Test 9 -> 10 bit transition
  - Test large repetitive files
  - Test that compressed then decompressed file is identical

- Compare stats
  - Compare `LZW1` fixed 16-bit version against `LZW2`
  - Check file size, compression ratio and percentage reduction
